{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "נקם נקמת בני ישראל", :translit "nekem nikmat b'nei yisra'el", :gloss "Avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (סמיכות) of parashiyot: our passage follows the parashah of vows"}], :frame {:text "The principle that juxtaposition of adjacent passages yields legal or homiletical instruction is a standard method of Midrashic exegesis (see Sifre Numbers 131 for juxtapositions in Sefer Bemidbar). Vows taken in times of distress appear in tractate Shabbat 32a. The obligation of a husband or father to annul vows is found in Numbers 30:3-16.", :sources ["Sifre Numbers 131" "Shabbat 32a" "Numbers 30:3-16"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אחר תאסף", :translit "achar te'asef", :gloss "Afterward you shall be gathered"}], :mechanisms [], :frame {:text "Ba'al HaTurim reasons from Moses's punishment being linked to the Midianite war: just as Moses was told he would die after this battle (Numbers 31:2), the juxtaposition itself implies a causal link between his response to the Midianite crisis and his being gathered to his people.", :sources ["Numbers 31:2" "Numbers 20:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אל עמיך", :translit "el amekha", :gloss "To your people"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 10, :occurrences []}], :frame {:text "The observation that Moses's death is referred to in the Torah ten times reflects the scribal-masoretic tradition of counting repeated expressions. The plene spelling with yod in this phrase serves as a mnemonic signal that the count is ten, since the letter yod has the numerical value of ten.", :sources ["Numbers 31:2" "Deuteronomy 32:50"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (סמיכות) of parashiyot: our passage follows the parashah of vows", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 10, :occurrences [], :by :selah, :located []}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:32:24.924489164Z", :model "glm-5.2"}